.TH FKALLOC 3 2026-09-24 "fkstring" "fkstring Library Manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
fkalloc, fkcalloc \- create an fkstring of a given length
.SH LIBRARY
fkstring library
.RI ( libfkstring ,\~ \-lfkstring )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fkstring.h>
.PP
.BI "fkstring *fkalloc(size_t " size );
.BI "fkstring *fkcalloc(size_t " nmemb ", size_t " size );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fkalloc ()
creates an
.B fkstring
whose length is
.I size
bytes.
The content bytes are uninitialized, for the caller to fill in, for example
with
.BR memcpy (3)
or
.BR read (2)
into
.IR fkcstr (s).
.PP
.BR fkcalloc ()
creates an
.B fkstring
whose length is
.IR nmemb "\~*\~" size
bytes, all set to zero.
They count as embedded NUL bytes, and
.BR fkstrlen ()
includes them.
.PP
In both cases the byte at offset
.I len
is a NUL terminator, and a length of 0 yields an empty
.B fkstring
with no buffer.
.PP
These are the way to preallocate a string: calling
.BR fkslack (3)
on an empty
.B fkstring
does nothing.
.SH RETURN VALUE
A new
.BR fkstring .
.SH ERRORS
These functions do not return on allocation failure.
Unlike
.BR calloc (3),
.BR fkcalloc ()
does not return NULL when
.IR nmemb "\~*\~" size
overflows; it terminates the process, as described in
.BR fkstring (3).
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
fkstring *s = fkalloc(n);
ssize_t   got = read(fd, fkcstr(s), n);
\&
if (got >= 0)
    fkstrtrunc(s, got);   /* keep what was read */
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fkstring (3),
.BR fkstrnew (3),
.BR fkslack (3),
.BR fkstrtrunc (3)
